    {
      "id": "7c184764-38ca-45ba-8a92-4ea54c40bcc4",
      "name": "MCP turns intent into action",
      "path": "WWW / MCP Journal / MCP turns intent into action",
      "docTypeId": "1c74e098-31f2-478f-9c13-75b3e17a1cf7",
      "templateId": "eec62926-28d6-4564-bdda-d6621700033e",
      "state": "Published",
      "domains": [],
      "values": [
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextArea",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "excerpt",
          "value": "MCP gives AI assistants a consistent way to discover tools and use them with the context they need. That simple bridge turns a request into a useful action."
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.BlockGrid",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "blocks",
          "value": {
            "layout": {
              "Umbraco.BlockGrid": [
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "3266ed1e-df70-4f7a-bf2e-39e8f6e7ff1b",
                  "settingsKey": null
                },
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "d9e2a40e-9937-4d85-bf74-198c019df746",
                  "settingsKey": null
                }
              ]
            },
            "contentData": [
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "25ac84b8-9614-4204-9f33-72f501b77d5c",
                "key": "3266ed1e-df70-4f7a-bf2e-39e8f6e7ff1b",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "heading",
                    "value": "MCP turns intent into action"
                  },
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextArea",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "intro",
                    "value": "A calmer way for people and software to work together."
                  },
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.MediaPicker3",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "image",
                    "value": [
                      {
                        "key": "5448ea74-74f6-4733-9442-bf8e55f212ca",
                        "mediaKey": "78ca2de3-61c1-4eba-be24-81524839d589",
                        "mediaTypeAlias": "Image",
                        "crops": [],
                        "focalPoint": null
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "85e5beaa-2ae4-4b16-a687-aa22ca6a3ae9",
                "key": "d9e2a40e-9937-4d85-bf74-198c019df746",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.RichText",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "body",
                    "value": {
                      "markup": "<p>Software gets more useful when it can reach the tools where work already happens. The Model Context Protocol (MCP) gives AI applications a common way to connect to those tools and the data around them.</p><h2>One connection, many capabilities</h2><p>Without a shared protocol, every assistant and service needs its own custom integration. MCP gives servers a predictable way to describe capabilities, so a client can discover what is available and call the right tool when a task needs it.</p><p>That means an assistant can move from explaining how to do something to actually doing it: look up a record, make a change, or check the result. The user can stay focused on the outcome while the tool handles the details.</p><h2>Context makes the difference</h2><p>A tool call is most helpful when it is grounded in the right information. MCP can expose resources and prompts alongside tools, giving the client a clearer picture of the system it is working with.</p><p>The result is a more natural loop: understand the request, inspect available context, take a bounded action, and report what happened. That is why MCP feels less like another chat feature and more like a practical interface to software.</p><p><em>Photo: Chris Ried / Unsplash.</em></p>",
                      "blocks": {
                        "layout": {},
                        "contentData": [],
                        "settingsData": [],
                        "expose": []
                      }
                    }
                  }
                ]
              }
            ],
            "settingsData": [],
            "expose": [
              {
                "contentKey": "3266ed1e-df70-4f7a-bf2e-39e8f6e7ff1b",
                "culture": null,
                "segment": null
              },
              {
                "contentKey": "d9e2a40e-9937-4d85-bf74-198c019df746",
                "culture": null,
                "segment": null
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ],
      "urls": [
        "/mcp-turns-intent-into-action/"
      ],
