    {
      "id": "46cde6c1-d537-4d1d-b927-e5e04a6b6261",
      "name": "One context for every tool",
      "path": "WWW / MCP Journal / One context for every tool",
      "docTypeId": "1c74e098-31f2-478f-9c13-75b3e17a1cf7",
      "templateId": "eec62926-28d6-4564-bdda-d6621700033e",
      "state": "Published",
      "domains": [],
      "values": [
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextArea",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "excerpt",
          "value": "MCP makes integrations easier to reuse. A consistent interface lets teams add new tools without rebuilding the entire assistant around each service."
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.BlockGrid",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "blocks",
          "value": {
            "layout": {
              "Umbraco.BlockGrid": [
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "d50a90d6-5bc9-40f1-91eb-e3c911ef6f88",
                  "settingsKey": null
                },
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "fea71684-a141-4a42-9222-95e8b682c6ba",
                  "settingsKey": null
                }
              ]
            },
            "contentData": [
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "25ac84b8-9614-4204-9f33-72f501b77d5c",
                "key": "d50a90d6-5bc9-40f1-91eb-e3c911ef6f88",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "heading",
                    "value": "One context for every tool"
                  },
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextArea",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "intro",
                    "value": "Open connections make capable assistants easier to extend."
                  },
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.MediaPicker3",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "image",
                    "value": [
                      {
                        "key": "25e3a0bb-337a-43e1-8eaa-b5b8f40d639a",
                        "mediaKey": "098864ff-b3a5-4d18-8ad1-ef5659078af1",
                        "mediaTypeAlias": "Image",
                        "crops": [],
                        "focalPoint": null
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "85e5beaa-2ae4-4b16-a687-aa22ca6a3ae9",
                "key": "fea71684-a141-4a42-9222-95e8b682c6ba",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.RichText",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "body",
                    "value": {
                      "markup": "<p>The promise of MCP is not only that an assistant can call tools. It is that those connections can be added in a consistent way, across different clients and different systems.</p><h2>Build the connection once</h2><p>A team can expose a useful capability through an MCP server, then make it available to compatible clients. The server describes the tools it offers and the inputs they expect. Clients can inspect that description and decide which capability fits the user's request.</p><p>This gives product teams room to specialize. A content system can expose content operations; a database can expose safe queries; a local development environment can expose project tools. Each one can focus on its own domain while the assistant works across them.</p><h2>Keep people in the loop</h2><p>Clear tool names and structured inputs make it easier to understand what an assistant is about to do. Systems can keep permissions and approval steps close to the action, while the client presents the result in the same conversation.</p><p>That combination—reusable connections, discoverable capabilities, and a clear record of the action—makes MCP an appealing foundation for useful automation. It lets the ecosystem grow one well-defined tool at a time.</p><p><em>Photo: Vardan Papikyan / Unsplash.</em></p>",
                      "blocks": {
                        "layout": {},
                        "contentData": [],
                        "settingsData": [],
                        "expose": []
                      }
                    }
                  }
                ]
              }
            ],
            "settingsData": [],
            "expose": [
              {
                "contentKey": "d50a90d6-5bc9-40f1-91eb-e3c911ef6f88",
                "culture": null,
                "segment": null
              },
              {
                "contentKey": "fea71684-a141-4a42-9222-95e8b682c6ba",
                "culture": null,
                "segment": null
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ],
